+### §check-fails-closed — a failing command stops the check; only the size warning is advisory
+- Since: 2026-10-23. The maintainer overrode the earlier §check-fails-open decision (2026-09-11), which forbade `set -e`
+  and any exit status other than 0.
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. A command that really fails (`git diff --cached`, `wc` on an unreadable file) aborts
+  the check with a non-zero exit. The large-file warning stays advisory: it goes to stderr and the check exits 0.
+- Forbidden: removing `-e` (or adding `set +e`), and `|| true`, `|| exit 0` or similar patterns that let a real failure
+  exit 0. Also a test such as `[ ... ] && echo` as the last command of the loop body: when the test is false it becomes
+  the loop's status, `pipefail` passes it on, and the check aborts on normal commits. Use `if ...; then ...; fi`.
+- Reason: with `set -e`, a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file once aborted the check and blocked every commit
+  in the repository for a day (2026-09-10). That case is still guarded: `[ -f "$f" ] || continue` skips files that
+  no longer exist, so they do not abort the check. Bash does not apply `-e` inside `$(...)`, so failures in the loop
+  reach the script only through the loop's exit status and `pipefail`.
